A bespoke animation-asset directory type should own `path_prefix`, e.g.
```rs
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(super) struct AnimationAssetDirectory(Cow<'static, str>);

impl AnimationAssetDirectory {
    pub(super) const HUMANOID_UNARMED: Self =
        Self(Cow::Borrowed("animations/biped/unarmed"));

    pub(super) const HUMANOID_2H_CLOSE: Self =
        Self(Cow::Borrowed("animations/biped/2h_close"));

    pub(super) fn motion_path(&self, motion: &MotionId) -> AnimationAssetPath {
        AnimationAssetPath::from(format!("{}/{motion}.glb", self.0))
    }
}
```
Then `PackBuilder` stores `asset_directory: AnimationAssetDirectory`, accepts that type in its constructor, and delegates filename construction to `motion_path`.

Its contract should be a nonempty, asset-root-relative directory, with trailing separators normalized and absolute paths, URI schemes and traversal rejected by any open constructor. Since `MotionId` supplies the filename stem, that admission also needs to prevent separators or traversal from defeating the directory contract.

String comparisons. The caller already has `kind: CalibrationMotion`; carry that into this operation:
```rs
fn clear_motion(&mut self, kind: CalibrationMotion) {
    self.phase_curves.remove(&kind.motion_id());

    match kind {
        CalibrationMotion::Walk => self.walk = None,
        CalibrationMotion::Run => self.run = None,
        CalibrationMotion::Strafe => self.strafe = None,
        CalibrationMotion::Skip => self.skip = None,
    }
}
```
Here, `motion_id()` would expose the conversion currently performed inside `CalibrationMotion::source()`. Move the existing enum to an owner accessible to both calibration and its cache.

*Bruno's Codex instance, writing on Bruno's behalf.*

**Keep one complete observation and model its action and actuator explicitly.** Keep initialization coupled to the retained observation, using `Option<QuickstepTraceObservation>` or an equivalent `Unobserved | Observed(...)` state. Within that observation, reuse `SkeletonAction` and place the actuator's tick inside its state:

```rust
struct QuickstepTraceObservation {
    action: SkeletonAction,
    actuator: ObservedQuickstepActuator,
}

enum ObservedQuickstepActuator {
    Inactive {
        retained_start_tick: AnimationSampleTick,
    },
    Active {
        start_tick: AnimationSampleTick,
    },
}
```

Preserve the existing change-detection and logging behavior. Whole-record derived equality on `SkeletonAction` would make unrelated action changes count as quickstep transitions; compare the relevant dodge membership explicitly instead. Retain the inactive start tick because `QuickstepPush::cancel` preserves it and the trace currently compares it.

Keep action and actuator observations independently representable: this diagnostic must still detect disagreement. An active actuator without a dodge animation can also be a legitimate attack lunge, so preserve that distinction when classifying discrepancies.

Carry `AnimationSampleTick` from the authoritative producer through `QuickstepPush.start_tick`, `QuickstepPush::begin` and their relevant consumers into this observation; do not erase it upstream and reconstruct it only in the trace. See the [producer-propagation rule](https://github.com/adventure-simulator-group/fabelgeist/blob/f0ba1326179fae1c345f83aa31ded5529ee5cd87/crates/AGENTS.md#L31-L42).
